p621:record_5d903167 a dataout:MonitoringRecord ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom p621:source_86139037 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy p621:MonitoringDec2025 ;
    dataout:concernsLegalArticle law:article_6_21_part_2 ;
    dataout:hasDefendant p621:official_0001 ;
    dataout:hasPenalty p621:pen_fine_9c573f39 ;
    dataout:hasSnippet "The editor-in-chief of the Kursk online media outlet “CRNews” was fined for publishing the article \"You and I won't make it\" (09.08.2013), which, according to the court, “contained information aimed at developing non-traditional sexual attitudes in minors.” A judicial expert determined that the article contained “a sufficient number of suggestive techniques to [...] influence the psyche of children in terms of the presence of propaganda of homosexualism.” The expert classified phrases from the article as: “direct suggestive commands” ('Why don't you try becoming a lesbian'); “truismes” ('There aren't many joys in the life of gays'); “programming homosexual behavior” ('I can become a good mother'); “phrases appealing to presubposition” ('you know that gays earn more'); “use of manipulative figures of speech” (the expert wrote that the sentence 'After all, the male body with which God mistakenly endowed me is more resilient' “contains the false premise that God can make mistakes”); “analogous phrases with a false premise” ('Two dads? What a shame. But what about a mother, a grandmother, an aunt – is that a normal family?!'). The article is no longer available online."@en ;
    dataout:hasTriggerObject p621:mass_media_002 ;
    dataout:monitoredIncidentYear "2014"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dataout:monitoringRecordNumber "4-а-75/2014" ;
    schema:addressRegion region:46 ;
    schema:identifier "record_5d903167" .

p621:record_5dc3ac97 a dataout:MonitoringRecord ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom p621:source_c9e35c3b ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy p621:MonitoringDec2025 ;
    dataout:concernsLegalArticle law:article_6_21_2_part_2 ;
    dataout:hasDefendant p621:1087746719906 ;
    dataout:hasPenalty p621:pen_fine_18717ce3 ;
    dataout:hasSnippet "“Universal Communications” was fined after the court found that its television channel “Hollywood” had broadcast two films — “The Infiltrator” (2016, Brad Furman) and “Official Competition” (2021, Mariano Cohn and Gastón Duprat) — to minors under a 16+ age rating. In relation to “The Infiltrator”, the court isolated a fragment stating that it contained “visual information corresponding to signs of the demonstration of non-traditional sexual relations or preferences.” For “Official Competition”, the judgment focused on a scene described as “a prolonged kiss between two persons of the same biological sex (female), expressing sexual feelings,” with the court adding that “other sexual actions are also demonstrated.” It then extended the analysis by classifying kisses into social and sensual categories, concluding that the scene belonged to the latter because it conveyed affection, tenderness, and love rather than neutral social contact. These quoted observations formed the basis for finding that both films demonstrated non-traditional sexual relations and therefore could not lawfully remain accessible to minors under a 16+ classification."@en ;
    dataout:hasTriggerObject p621:325789,
        p621:668640 ;
    dataout:monitoredIncidentYear "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dataout:monitoringRecordNumber "05-6774/2023" ;
    schema:addressRegion region:77 ;
    schema:identifier "record_5dc3ac97" .

p621:record_5e7787b8 a dataout:MonitoringRecord ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom p621:source_e52768d3 ;
    prov:wasGeneratedBy p621:MonitoringDec2025 ;
    dataout:concernsLegalArticle law:article_6_21_part_2 ;
    dataout:hasDefendant p621:individual_0012 ;
    dataout:hasPenalty p621:pen_fine_3596fae3 ;
    dataout:hasSnippet "Yuliya Tsvetkova, an artist and activist from Komsomolsk-on-Amur, was fined for two social media posts expressing support for young subscribers of the VKontakte group “The Last Supper – LGBTQIAP+on-Amur” accompanied by images of cartoon cats coloured in queer flags. Yulia appealed the court decision twice, but the cassation court upheld the punishment."@en ;
    dataout:hasTriggerObject p621:social_media_010 ;
